    "detailed_description": "<p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >Love You More is 3D first person narrative game in which you play as Connor, a single</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >father, who visits a memory-restoration clinic in order to relive the days he had with his</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >now-estranged son Bry. In the game, you control Connor as he gets to relive his cherished</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >memories and routine joys, while being faced with the darker parts of how his actions</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >shaped their distant relationship.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >As you go through their old house, you will explore fragments of Connor\u2019s memories with his</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >son, from packing his lunch to decorating the house for his birthday party. Each of them</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >unfolds a story through its own unique mechanic, meant to immerse the player into the</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >feeling of nostalgia and comfort.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >At one point, something shifts. A gap in your memory the simulation skips over. It grows</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >wider, swallowing more and more. Connor does not even see it. What will be left of your</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >son\u2019s memory in the end?</p>",
    "about_the_game": "<p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >Love You More is 3D first person narrative game in which you play as Connor, a single</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >father, who visits a memory-restoration clinic in order to relive the days he had with his</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >now-estranged son Bry. In the game, you control Connor as he gets to relive his cherished</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >memories and routine joys, while being faced with the darker parts of how his actions</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >shaped their distant relationship.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >As you go through their old house, you will explore fragments of Connor\u2019s memories with his</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >son, from packing his lunch to decorating the house for his birthday party. Each of them</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >unfolds a story through its own unique mechanic, meant to immerse the player into the</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >feeling of nostalgia and comfort.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >At one point, something shifts. A gap in your memory the simulation skips over. It grows</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >wider, swallowing more and more. Connor does not even see it. What will be left of your</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >son\u2019s memory in the end?</p>",
